<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.MakingLanguagesInRust.Posts[0]">
    <Paragraph>
        I've been recently learning MLIR, which promises a more, let's say, <em>sane</em> way to write intermediate
        representations and compilers. It has one important disadvantage, though, which is that it's written in
        C++; not only that, it is deeply entwined with C++ and its idioms such as inheritance and template magic.
        So I asked the obvious question &ndash; <Highlight>how viable is it to use MLIR from Rust?</Highlight>
        To that end, I implemented the MLIR Toy Tutorial<Footnote IsRef="true">
            <LinkA Href="https://mlir.llvm.org/docs/Tutorials/Toy/">MLIR Docs: Toy Tutorial</LinkA>
        </Footnote> with the explicit goal of minimising pain and lines of C++ written.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        While there are ways to use MLIR outside of C++ &ndash; MLIR exposes a C API and we will be making extensive use
        of it through Melior<Footnote IsRef="true">
            <LinkA Href="https://github.com/mlir-rs/melior">Github: mlir-rs/melior</LinkA>
        </Footnote>
        &ndash; they very quickly turned out
        to be&hellip; lackluster, to say the least. It also doesn't help that MLIR's documentation is overall sparse
        and,
        frankly, quite terrible. It almost feels as if the docs assume you're already familiar with MLIR and just need
        a refresher.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>Target Audience</Header2>

    <Paragraph>
        Unfortunately I am (was?) not familiar with MLIR, or the underlying LLVM framework. I mean, I know what LLVM
        <em>is</em>, I indirectly used it all my life, I even saw LLVM IR somewhere on a slide, but I never used it as a
        library. This series of blog posts is targeting a similar demographic &ndash;
        <Highlight>people who are interested
            in making programming languages and compilers, who kinda know how a compiler is shaped, but have little or
            no
            experience with LLVM and MLIR.
        </Highlight>
        And, obviously, who prefer writing Rust to C++, because otherwise
        what's the purpose of the exercise.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        If you're not that familiar with building programming languages, my sincere recommendation is to take a look at
        the (free!) Crafting Interpreters book
        by Robert Nystrom.<Footnote IsRef="true"><LinkA Href="https://craftinginterpreters.com/">Robert Nystrom, Crafting Interpreters</LinkA></Footnote>
        It's an excellent introduction to the PL world, and although it doesn't touch on compilers it is nonetheless
        valuable
        &ndash; there's a reason we teach students interpreters first.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        If you're already an MLIR expert but are nonetheless interested in using it from Rust then this will be valuable
        as well, you will probably just be annoyed at me being dumb and not understanding MLIR.
        If you decide to read through I'd appreciate your feedback in the comments, especially if you find a way
        to simplify some of the wizardry I had to perform to make this work.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>Goals</Header2>

    <Paragraph>
        I want to recreate the full Toy Tutorial but in Rust. Toy is, well, a toy language that allows some simple
        numerical
        operations and illustrates the power of MLIR and its custom dialects quite well. You don't need to know the
        original
        tutorial, I want to make this series self-contained.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        I am not interested in settling ideological disputes, so let's make one thing perfectly clear at the outset:
        <b>Rust good; C++ bad</b>. You may disagree, and I may not care. The goal of this exercise is to put as much of
        the required logic as possible into Rust, and use C++ as little as possible. In particular, from my perspective
        there are four kinds of systems-level code, in decreasing order of preference:

        <OList Dense="true" Class="my-2">
            <ListItem Icon="fas fa-1">Rust code &ndash; this one sparks joy;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="fas fa-2">C++ code generated by tools;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="fas fa-3">C++ code written by someone else and packaged in a library;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="fas fa-4">C++ code I have to write &ndash; does not spark joy.</ListItem>
        </OList>

        In this tutorial I am treating the Rust project as the core codebase, and MLIR and the C++ API as an incidental
        necessity.
        We want as much Type 1 code and as little Type 4 code as possible.
        If we can make meaningful contributions to Melior or MLIR along the way to make using it outside of C++
        easier, that's great.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>Chapters</Header2>

    <Paragraph>
        The original tutorial has seven chapters. This one will probably have more, but it is work in progress.
        The rough plan is this (highlighted means the blog post is published):
        <OList Dense="true" Class="my-2">
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
                <LinkA Href="/sorcery/making-languages-in-rust-chapter-1">
                    Chapter 1: Toy Language Definition and lalrpop Parser
                </LinkA>;
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
                <LinkA Href="/sorcery/making-languages-in-rust-chapter-2">
                    Chapter 2: Basic MLIR Codegen
                </LinkA>;
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">
                <LinkA Href="/sorcery/making-languages-in-rust-chapter-3">
                    Chapter 3: Sweeping C++ Under the Carpet
                </LinkA>;
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 4: Snapshot Testing;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 5: MLIR Canonicalization Passes;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 6: Hooking into MLIR Generic Interface
                Transformations;
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 7: Lowering to Builtin Dialects;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 8: Lowering to LLVM, Codegen, JIT;</ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 9: Improving Rust TableGen Generation;
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ChevronRight">Chapter 10: Extending Toy with a Composite Type.
            </ListItem>
        </OList>
    </Paragraph>

